{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 32, :comments ["<b>It is a land that eats its inhabitants.</b> For they saw them burying dead, and the Elohim accounted it for good, etc. And the question is difficult: is it on account of this that they were called 'those who brought out an evil report'? After all, they told the truth of what they saw. Is it merely because they did not perceive that HaShem did this for their benefit that they are called 'those who brought out an evil report'? They deserve rather to be called fools. And so the Midrash (Bemidbar Rabbah 16:2) concludes: 'The spies whom Moses sent were fools,' etc. And at the very least, one could say that his foolishness served him well — that he was neither sick nor felt any pain from the benefit [done for him]." "And it appears that they ought at the very least to have attributed the burials to the sin of the deceased. For it was already known to them that the deeds of the land of Canaan were more corrupt than those of all the other nations. But they said the opposite of this — that all the people whom we saw in its midst are men of good measure and righteous — and they attributed the matter to the bad temperament of the air of the land, which consumes and wastes away even its inhabitants who are accustomed to it. All the more so would it harm those who do not dwell upon it and who came from afar and were not accustomed to the air of the land. And in their saying 'men of good measure,' several matters were included: namely their righteousness and the uprightness of their dealings, in accurate measure and weight, not deviating from the line of fairness; and likewise the matter of their conduct in their eating and drinking, for they are not among those who indulge excessively in food and drink and marital relations, but rather they manage their affairs with proper discipline, in measure and at the fitting time. And if so, then surely everything depends on the heat of the air that corrupts health. 'And this is its fruit': for in a place where the heat is great, its fruit is extraordinary in excellence but its people are extraordinary in deficiency, for the one depends on the other. 'And there we saw the Nefilim' — from those who fell and died, who were cut down before their time. 'The sons of Anak, from the Nefilim' of old — and this is the duplication of 'the Nefilim,' for one is an epithet for the giants and one denotes literal falling. And they said: if the temperament of the air harms even those who are coarse of substance and strong, even when healthy, all the more so will it harm us, who are weak and small by comparison. 'And we were in our own eyes as grasshoppers, and so we were in their eyes' — for we heard them saying: there are ants in the vineyard. For seeing from afar is not like seeing from close up, for from afar everything appears smaller than it appears from close up, and grasshoppers are larger than ants. And if we were as grasshoppers in our own eyes, presumably from afar we were as ants in their eyes, for the one and the other is a single measure." "And Joshua and Caleb said to them: the land is exceedingly, exceedingly good — both in respect of its fruit and in respect of the temperament of its air. And the goodness of the fruits does not depend on the heat of the air but rather on the fattiness of the earth, which gives sweetness to the fruits. And this is what they said: 'a land flowing with milk and honey,' for milk is a designation for what the ground-fed beast yields, and honey is a designation for the sweetness that comes from the oils of the earth. And the air harms none but sinners. 'Only against HaShem do not rebel' — and then the air of the land will not harm you. 'And do not fear the people of the land' — for they were burying their dead. 'For they are our bread' — they surely died through their own sin, for it is written (Deuteronomy 7:16): 'And you shall consume all the peoples.' And the Sages said (Bava Kamma 38a): What is the meaning of what is written (Habakkuk 3:6), 'He saw and loosened the nations'? The Holy One, blessed be He, saw that they did not fulfill even the seven commandments of the children of Noah, and He arose and loosened their wealth for Israel. And if He loosened their wealth because they did not keep the seven commandments, then surely He also loosened their very bodies on account of all the great abominations that were among them. And if so, they surely died through their own sin — for if not, why did He permit us to consume them like bread? And a proof of their sin: even a single righteous man who was among them died through their sin, in order that he not shield them like a shadow. This is what they said: 'Their shadow has departed from over them.' But our shadow endures, and it is the Holy One, blessed be He, as it is written (Psalms 121:5): 'YHWH is your guardian; YHWH is your shadow.'" "And it is plausible to hear 'they are our bread' — לַחְמֵנוּ with a lamed holem — as לוֹחֲמֵינוּ, 'those who wage war against us,' for they are the masters of our warfare, and perhaps they would have perceived you and waged war against you. Therefore HaShem, blessed be He, arranged that they would be burying their dead, so that they would not set their hearts against you. And in Toldot Yitzchak he wrote: 'they are our bread' is like the manna — if its shade departs from it and the sun shines upon it, it melts; so the shadow of the Holy One, blessed be He, departed from them, and then they melted as wax before the fire, as it is written (Exodus 15:15): 'All the inhabitants of Canaan melted away.' And by way of allusion he said: 'Their shadow has departed from over them' — lest they say: if the nature of that land is that it vomits out its sinful inhabitants, today or tomorrow we too shall sin, for 'there is no righteous man on earth,' and so on, and it will vomit us out as well. To this he said: 'and YHWH is with us' — even in exile, as it is written (Psalms 91:15): 'I am with him in trouble.' And along with that, we too are assured that we shall return there, and the Holy One, blessed be He, for the sake of His name, blessed be He, who is with us, will once again redeem us."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:41:26.954885600Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}